			"width":260,
			"height":90,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"40ab0fb236a1f7cf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Feature Engineering",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1765,
			"y":583,
			"width":210,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"318b6774a3a7674f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Risk-Adjusted Deep Reinforcement Learning for Portfolio Optimization: A Multi-reward Approach\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":40,
			"y":-400,
			"width":1180,
			"height":780
		},
		{
			"id":"4b7dd767e0284c31",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Risk-Sensitive Deep Reinforcement Learning for Portfolio Optimization\n\nAn adaptive risk-sensitive transformer-based DRL framework to improve portfolio optimization in commodity futures (petroleum futures) training and demonstrates resilience by learning from historical periods of extreme volatility\n\n## Method 1:\nSingle DRL agent with following algorithms tested:\n\n- DQN\n    \n- PPO\n    \n- A2C\n    \n- DDPG\n\n## Method 2:\nIndependent DRL agents per asset\n\n## Method 3:\nAdaptive framework - system chooses best performing agent dynamically",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1280,
			"y":-400,
			"width":1180,
			"height":780
		},
		{
			"id":"9ea0885f61d5124e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Drawdown\nDrawdown measures how much the portfolio falls from its peak value.\nMaximum drawdown (MDD) =  largest drop from peak to bottom\t",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":420,
			"y":-880,
			"width":480,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"06a1e9396314d27f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"\n# Smart Tangency Portfolio: Deep Reinforcement Learning for Dynamic Rebalancing and Risk–Return Trade-Off\n\nThe system trains an AI agent that:\n\n1. Observes the **market** \n2. Decides **portfolio weights**\n3. Gets rewarded if **risk-adjusted return improves**\n4. Learns better strategies over time\n\nThe paper proposes a hybrid DRL-optimization framework where a reinforcement learning agent dynamically chooses portfolio risk level and rebalancing frequency, while classical mean-risk optimization computes portfolio weights, resulting in adaptive portfolios that outperform static tangency strategies.\n\n![[Pasted image 20260306141224.png]]\n\n# MDP Formulation Used in the Paper\n\n## 1. State Space (S)\n\nThe **state represents the current market and portfolio condition**.\n\nThe paper defines the observation/state as:\n\n$S_t=Box(f,n,t)$\n\nwhere:\n\n- **n** = number of securities\n- **f** = number of features per security\n- **t** = time window of past data\n\nSo the state contains a **3D tensor of market features**.\n\n### Features Used in the State\n\nThe state includes **technical indicators + price information** for each asset.\n\nExamples of features:\n\n- Adjusted close price\n- Percentage change in price\n- RSI\n- MACD\n- Bollinger bands\n- Moving averages (10, 20, 100)\n- Momentum indicators\n- Volume indicators\n- Z-score of price\n- Linear regression trend\n\nTotal:\n\n**34 technical indicators + price features**.\n### Intuition\n\nThe state tells the agent:\n\ncurrent market condition  \ntrend  \nmomentum  \nvolatility  \nvolume dynamics\n\nfor every asset in the portfolio.\n\n# 2. Action Space (A)\n\nThe agent **does NOT choose portfolio weights**.\n\nInstead it chooses **two control parameters**:\n\n$A_t=(λ_t,h_t)$\n### Action 1: Risk-Aversion Level (λ)\n\n$λ_t∈{1,2,...,100}$\n\nThis determines **which point on the efficient frontier to choose**.\n\nMeaning:\n\n|λ value|Behavior|\n|---|---|\n|small|conservative|\n|large|aggressive|\n\n### Action 2: Rebalancing Horizon (h)\n\n$h_t∈[5,60]$\nThis means:\n\n**how many trading days until the next rebalance**.\n\nExample:\n\nh = 5  → rebalance weekly  \nh = 60 → rebalance quarterly\n\nSo the action is:\n\n$A_t$ = (risk level , rebalance interval)\n\n# 3. Transition Function (P)\n\nThe transition represents **how the market evolves**.\n\nFormally:\n\n$P(S_{t+1}∣S_t,A_t)$\n\nIn this environment:\n\n1. Portfolio weights are computed by the optimizer.\n2. Portfolio is held for **h days**.\n3. Market prices change according to real historical data.\n4. New market state is observed.\n\nSo the transition is governed by:\n\nmarket returns  \nasset price evolution  \ntechnical indicator updates\n\n# 4. Reward Function (R)\n\nReward is **portfolio return during the holding period**.\n\nDefined as:\n\n$Rt=log⁡(PV_tPV_{t−h})R_t = \\log \\left(\\frac{PV_t}{PV_{t-h}}\\right)R_t​=log(PV_t−h​PV_t​​)$\n\nwhere\n\n- $PV_t$ = portfolio value after holding period\n- $PV_{t−h}​$ = portfolio value before rebalance\n\nSo reward = **log portfolio return**.\n\n### Why Log Return?\n\nLog returns:\n\n- additive across time\n- stable numerically\n- standard in finance\n\n# 5. Policy (π)\n\nThe policy is the **DRL agent** (PPO or A2C).\n\nIt learns:\n\n$π(A_t∣S_t)$\n\nMeaning:\n\ngiven market state  \nchoose optimal (λ , h)\n\n# 6. Discount Factor (γ)\n\nThe cumulative objective:\n\n$\\max E\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T} \\gamma^t R_t\\right]$\n\nThis means the agent learns a policy maximizing **long-term portfolio growth**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1200,
			"y":-400,
			"width":1180,
			"height":780
		},
		{
			"id":"990fcd3c45a96d30",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# RL Algorithms (Seen in the Papers)\n\n### **DQN (Deep Q-Network)**\n\nA value-based RL algorithm that learns Q-values using deep neural networks for discrete action spaces.\n\n---\n\n### **PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization)**\n\nA policy-gradient algorithm that updates policies gradually to ensure stable learning.\n\n---\n\n### **A2C (Advantage Actor-Critic)**\n\nUses two networks: an **actor** (policy) and a **critic** (value estimator) to improve training stability.\n\n---\n\n### **DDPG (Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient)**\n\nAn actor-critic algorithm designed for **continuous action spaces**, often used for portfolio weight allocation.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":940,
			"y":-880,
			"width":930,
			"height":420
		},
		{
			"id":"919dc13879a3f4d4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## modern portfolio theory\noptimize expected return for a given level of risk, emphasizing diversification to reduce volatility without necessarily sacrificing returns.\nAssumption: Investors are rational and risk-averse\nMathematical basis: Mean-variance optimization (mean is expected return and variance is risk)\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1200,
			"y":-880,
			"width":560,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"2f49aeb9ea8fd4ec",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## tangency portfolio\nportfolio with best return-risk ratio\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-610,
			"y":-880,
			"width":350,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f5151c3f37975e6b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Sharpe Ratio\nThe Sharpe Ratio measures how much return you get for the risk you take.\n\nA high Sharpe ratio means:\n\tgood returns\n\tlow volatility\n\nA low Sharpe ratio means:\n\treturns are unstable\n\thigh risk for little reward.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-220,
			"y":-880,
			"width":600,
			"height":320,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"bc788f028f846147",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"### Liquidity Buffer Calculator  \n# $E_t = \\frac {Cash} {Monthly Expenses}$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":615,
			"y":3010,
			"width":410,
			"height":140
		},
